* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.woff2 binary
# RFC 5545 requires CRLF line endings; the blanket `eol=lf` rule above would
# silently renormalise them to LF on every commit otherwise. It is the one
# file of this shape this repository ever commits.
instance/public-data/agenda-internal.ics binary

# `declarations/boundary.yml` declares this path `regenerated:` -- rewritten in
# full from the commit history by `.github/workflows/derive-decision-register.yml`, in
# every instance including this one. Upstream's rows and a duplicate's rows
# are both correct, entirely different, and rewritten on both sides between
# any two merges, so git sees two sides that changed the same lines and
# raises a conflict whose only correct resolution is "mine". This is that
# resolution, written down. It needs the driver configured once per clone
# (`git config merge.ours.driver true`); without it git falls back to the
# ordinary conflict, which is the state this replaces.
# `tools/tests/declaration/test_boundary.py` refuses a `regenerated:` path
# that is missing this line.
docs/handbook/governance/register.md merge=ours

# The three directories `declarations/boundary.yml` gives the instance.
#
# That declaration says upstream does not *edit* them. It cannot say upstream
# does not *run*: this repository is itself a running instance, and its own
# scheduled jobs commit into all three. `sweep-and-notify.yml` writes
# `speakers.yml`, `queue-watch.yml`, `queue-ledger.yml` and
# `actions-usage.yml`; `retention.yml` writes `retention-last-run.yml`,
# `event-key-destructions.yml` and `instance/keys/events`; `deploy.yml` writes
# all three projections under `public-data`; `candidate-form.yml`,
# `invite-survey.yml`, `registration.yml` and the certificate workflows write
# more. Every one is a commit a duplicate's next merge has to do something
# with.
#
# **And the bad outcome is not a conflict.** Measured on a duplicate one
# release behind: upstream's own edits merged into the duplicate's
# `instance/data/speakers.yml` with *no* conflict at all, because the header
# and the records occupy different regions of the file and git applied
# upstream's hunks in silence. A conflict would have been the good outcome --
# loud, and a person gets to decide. `docs/operating/taking-an-update.md` promised these
# paths never conflict, which was true when nothing upstream had run yet and
# read as a promise that nothing arrives.
#
# So this is the resolution written down, and it is the same one the register
# above gets for the same reason: a duplicate's records are the duplicate's.
# It does not stop a *new* file arriving -- `merge=ours` is consulted only
# when both sides changed the same path, and the same measured merge created
# `instance/data/queue-watch.yml` normally.
#
# Needs `git config merge.ours.driver true`, once per clone, like the register
# above. Without it git falls back to the ordinary conflict, which is strictly
# better than the silence but is not what this asks for, so the page an
# operator reads before merging says to run it.
instance/data/** merge=ours
instance/keys/** merge=ours
instance/public-data/** merge=ours

# Except the files upstream keeps inside those directories, which
# `boundary.yml` declares `kept:` and an instance never edits. `!merge` leaves
# the attribute unspecified -- the ordinary three-way merge -- rather than
# `-merge`, which would mean "take ours and declare a conflict" and is the
# opposite of the intent.
instance/data/schema.md !merge
instance/keys/events/README.md !merge
instance/keys/signing/README.md !merge
instance/public-data/README.md !merge
